# Rendering performance
@scenario tutorial_lesson_28
@include /definitions/characters.vns
@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[bg room]
[char ginger global on]
[set ui.dialogueFadeMs 160]
[stage clear]
[show ginger guide thoughtful]
ginger: Use the {b}Render inspector{/b} in the Run workspace to compare cache entries, hits and misses. Make a note of the values while this pose is displayed.
ginger: Advance to change the pose, then advance again to return to the original one. Compare the counters after each change.
[show ginger guide normal]
ginger: The sprite now uses another expression. It may already be resident if you opened another lesson earlier.
[show ginger guide thoughtful]
ginger: The first pose is back. A hit means a lookup reused an entry. A miss means the requested entry was unavailable. Other assets can affect the totals too.
ginger: For a lighting example in the engine checkout, run scripts/examples/window-light/scene.vns. Observe repeated frames with the same rig before changing a light.
ginger: An entry count describes what is resident. Hit and miss counts describe lookup events. Several requests can hit one resident entry, so those numbers measure different things.
ginger: Take a counter reading before and after a defined sequence of poses. Use the difference between readings. A total accumulated since startup also includes earlier lessons.
> Read the cache counters -> practice
> Review question -> check
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label check
ginger: When is a cached lighting result safe to reuse?
> The previous image has the same filename alone. -> retry
> The source, rig and relevant dimensions still match. -> correct
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label retry
[show ginger guide confused]
ginger: Moving a light changes the image even if the source filename stays the same. Include the inputs that affect the rendered result.
[jump check]
@label correct
[show ginger guide normal]
ginger: Reuse the result when the relevant source, lighting rig and dimensions still match.
> Back to topics -> return_hub
> Replay example -> start
@label return_hub
[if !tutorial_done_28]
[flag tutorial_done_28]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[stage clear]
[goto TutorialHub:topics_page_6]
[end]

@label practice
[show ginger guide thoughtful]
ginger: Start from a fresh runtime and record counters after the first pose settles. Advance to the second pose, record again, then return to the first pose and take a third reading.
ginger: A new pose may trigger a miss and create an entry. Revisiting it can hit if the relevant entry remains resident. Backgrounds, composites and stage passes can produce other lookups at the same time.
ginger: Repeat the sequence in the same window at the same size. Then run a separate comparison that changes one lighting input. Keep the pose sequence identical between the two measurements.
ginger: A higher hit count does not by itself establish a higher frame rate. Record frame timing over the same interval if performance is the question. Inspect the actual image if correctness is the question.
@label practice_question
ginger: Hits rise from 120 to 138 during the measured section. How many hits belong to that interval?
> 138 distinct assets. -> practice_hint
> 18 lookups. -> practice_result
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label practice_hint
ginger: 138 is the accumulated total. It includes earlier activity and does not tell you how many distinct assets were requested.
[jump practice_question]
@label practice_result
ginger: Subtract the starting counter from the ending counter. The result counts lookup events, and several can refer to a single resident asset.
> Review question -> check
> Try the exercise again -> practice
> Back to topics -> return_hub
